Short explanations for the 'skyax' package: (status 9. January 2023)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The following instructions assume that you are on a Linux system.

Create a working directory 'SkyAx' and change to it. Place the
the package 'SkyAx.tgz' there and unpack it. This will generate 
the documentation file "Skyax.pdf" and the subdirectories:
source_f90         contains all source files and a 'Makefile', the
                   latter provides options for GNU Fortran and 
                   Intel Fortran;
doc-doxygen        contains sources for 'doxygen' and its actual output
                   on 'SkyAx_doxygen.pdf' which delivers more detailed
                   information from the various subroutines.
                   (The doxygen output has yet to be updated.)
test_cases_Skyrme  Skyrme test cases input and output.
test_cases_Fayans  Fayans test cases input and output.

To compile and link change to subdirectory 'source_f90' and edit the
'Makefile'. Two compilers are provided, Intel Fortran 'ifort' and
GNU Fortran 'gfortran'. Check the 'COMPILERFLAGS' such that they
comply with your installation.
Once you have updated the 'Makefile' just issue a 'make'. That
will tell you the usage. For example, if you want to use 'gfortran'
just execute 'make ifort'. This will place an executable
'skyax.ifort.seq' in the working directory (one level above 'source').

A simple way to run the code is to choose one of the functionals
provided in the file 'source_f90/forces.data'. Examples for input are
given in the subdirectories of the directories 'test_cases_*' with name
starting by 'for005'. All these test cases contains input files and
corresponding output files to check the actual installation. 

To specify an example:  take 'Cd118' where the input file is named
'for005'. Go to a working directory of your choice, copy 'for005' from
'Ca48' to that and run '~/SkyAx/skyax.ifort.seq < for005'. This
will produce several output files with the qualifier 'tst' (defined in
'for005') from which the 'ensurf.tst' contains compressed information
on deformation, energy, radii etc. The file 'out_details.tst' shows a
detailed protocol of iteration and results. For more explanations and
the meaning of other output files see the documentation in 'SkyAx.pdf'.


There is still one obnoxious feature. The results depend slightly on
the compiler used. The worst case is Intel Fortran with OpenMP 
(option 'make ifort_omp') which can fail badly in some cases. This
indicates that there is probably some non-standard use of Fortran
constructs which deserves further checking.
 